NuScale Power (NYSE: SMR)NuScale holds the distinction of being the only advanced SMR developer with cleared NRC design certifications.NRC Certification & Approval Details:50 MWe Design Certification: Certified by the NRC in January 2023 under 10 CFR Part 52 rulemaking for its 50 MWe (160 MWt) NuScale Power Module, valid for 40 years.77 MWe Standard Design Approval: Achieved formal Standard Design Approval (SDA) on May 28–29, 2025, for its uprated 77 MWe (250 MWt) module design (US460 configuration). The NRC's Final Safety Evaluation Report (FSER) was completed ahead of schedule, confirming the safety case for uprated passive cooling.Non-US Approvals: Obtained a formal regulatory safety evaluation from Poland's National Atomic Energy Agency (PAA), confirming that its 77 MWe design complies with Polish nuclear safety standards.Oklo Inc. (NYSE: OKLO)Oklo currently holds no active NRC design certifications, standard design approvals, construction permits, or combined licenses.Regulatory History & Current Stage:In January 2022, the NRC formally denied Oklo's 2020 Combined License Application (COLA) for its 1.5 MWe Aurora plant at Idaho National Laboratory (INL) without prejudice due to incomplete safety cases, missing transient analyses, and unverified thermal-hydraulic modeling.Oklo is operating in pre-application status, submitting licensing topical reports and safety white papers to re-establish a baseline. While Oklo was selected in August 2026 for Department of Energy (DOE) authorization frameworks on federal sites, commercial powerhouse deployment requires formal NRC docketing and review.Non-US Approvals: None.X-Energy, Inc. (NASDAQ: XE)X-Energy does not hold a full reactor Design Certification, but its commercial fuel plant holds an active license, and its reactor design is actively docketed under a Construction Permit review.NRC Stage: Operating under a 10 CFR Part 50 two-step licensing process.Xe-100 Reactor: The Construction Permit (CP) application for its lead commercial project with Dow Chemical at Seadrift, Texas, was formally docketed by the NRC in May 2025 under an expedited 18-month target review schedule aiming for a Final Safety Evaluation Report (FSER) by late 2026.TX-1 Fuel Facility: Received a commercial nuclear fuel fabrication facility license from the NRC for its TRISO-X (TX-1) facility in Oak Ridge, Tennessee.Non-US Approvals: Active in Vendor Design Review (VDR) pre-application interactions with the Canadian Nuclear Safety Commission (CNSC).Nano Nuclear Energy Inc. (NASDAQ: NNE)Nano Nuclear holds no regulatory approvals from the NRC or international bodies.NRC Stage: Pre-application status. The NRC established pre-application Project No. 99902094 for NNE's KRONOS high-temperature gas microreactor and ZEUS solid-core microreactor. On March 31, 2026, the University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign submitted a Part 50 Construction Permit application for an academic research reactor incorporating NNE components. Proprietary commercial NNE microreactors remain un-docketed.Non-US Approvals: None.Terrestrial Energy Inc. (NASDAQ: IMSR)Terrestrial Energy holds no formal NRC approvals but leads the peer group in Canadian regulatory progression.NRC Stage: Pre-application status. Submitted its foundational Safety Analysis Report to the US NRC in July 2026 to align on principal design criteria for the Integral Molten Salt Reactor (IMSR).Non-US Approvals: Successfully completed Phase 1 and Phase 2 Vendor Design Reviews (VDR) with the Canadian Nuclear Safety Commission (CNSC), establishing a regulatory baseline for Canadian commercial deployment.2. Fuel Supply Chains and Critical Point-of-Failure AnalysisFuel enrichment requirements establish a defining operational divide. Advanced fast reactors and high-temperature gas reactors require High-Assay Low-Enriched Uranium (HALEU), enriched between 5% and 19.75% U-235. Because Western commercial HALEU enrichment is presently limited to demonstration-scale output (~900 kg annually from Centrus Energy), HALEU-dependent developers face severe supply-chain exposure.ENRICHMENT REQUIREMENTS AND BOTTLENECK EXPOSURE

